QB.push(
{i:171,d:2,t:"2.1",s:"CSA",diff:2,
q:"Your agent frequently calls <code>get_customer</code> when the user asks an order-related question that <code>lookup_order</code> should handle. Both tool descriptions are one short line and use similar wording about &ldquo;retrieving account information&rdquo;.",
ask:"What is the first thing you should do?",
a:["Expand each description to state its input formats, example queries, edge cases and applicability boundaries so the two are clearly distinguishable",
"Add few-shot examples to the system prompt showing correct tool selection",
"Add a routing layer that classifies the request before the agent sees it",
"Merge the two tools into a single <code>get_account_data</code> tool"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. Tool descriptions are the model's primary selection mechanism; this is the lowest-effort, highest-impact fix and it targets the stated root cause.",
"Few-shot adds tokens on every request without addressing why the descriptions are ambiguous. Useful later, not first.",
"A routing layer is over-engineering for a metadata problem, and it adds latency and a second component to maintain.",
"Merging is more effort than justified and produces a broader tool, which brings its own selection and validation problems."],
e:"This mirrors a published sample question, and its reasoning is worth memorising verbatim: the description is the primary mechanism the model uses to select a tool, so minimal or overlapping descriptions produce unreliable selection. A good description states what the tool does, what inputs it accepts and in what format, example queries that should route to it, edge cases, and &mdash; crucially &mdash; the boundary against the nearest alternative (&ldquo;use <code>lookup_order</code> for anything keyed by order number or order status; use <code>get_customer</code> only for profile and account-level data&rdquo;).",
k:"Tool descriptions are the primary selection signal. Fix ambiguity there first: inputs, examples, edge cases, and the boundary against similar tools."},

{i:172,d:2,t:"2.1",s:"MAR",diff:3,
q:"Two tools exist: <code>analyze_content</code> (which actually fetches and parses web search results) and <code>analyze_document</code> (which parses uploaded PDFs). The agent routinely calls <code>analyze_content</code> on PDFs.",
ask:"Which change is most effective?",
a:["Rename <code>analyze_content</code> to <code>extract_web_results</code> so the name itself conveys the boundary, and update the description accordingly",
"Add a validation error to <code>analyze_content</code> when it receives a PDF",
"Add a system prompt rule about which tool to use for PDFs",
"Merge both tools into a single <code>analyze</code> tool that detects the input type"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. The name was actively misleading &mdash; &ldquo;content&rdquo; sounds more general than &ldquo;document&rdquo;. Naming is part of the selection signal.",
"Catching the mistake is better than silently producing garbage, but it wastes a turn every time and never fixes the misrouting.",
"A prompt rule patches over a misleading name that will keep misleading the model in every new context.",
"Merging removes the misrouting by removing the choice, but a general-purpose tool is harder to describe, validate and reason about &mdash; the exam's stated preference is specialised tools with clear contracts."],
e:"The blueprint gives this exact example: rename <code>analyze_content</code> to <code>extract_web_results</code> to eliminate functional overlap. Names carry semantic weight in selection, often more than the description body, because a vague name creates an association the description then has to fight. Prefer specific, boundary-revealing names, and when two tools overlap, fix the naming and descriptions before reaching for routing layers or merges.",
k:"Tool names are part of the selection signal. Rename to eliminate overlap before adding routing, rules or validation workarounds."},

{i:173,d:2,t:"2.1",s:"AAT",diff:2,
q:"A general-purpose <code>fetch_url</code> tool is used by a research agent to retrieve documents. It is also occasionally used to hit internal APIs, which was never intended.",
ask:"Which change best addresses this?",
a:["Replace it with a constrained <code>load_document</code> tool that only accepts document sources, narrowing both the contract and the capability",
"Add a description note asking the agent not to use it for internal APIs",
"Add a deny-list of internal hostnames to the tool description",
"Keep the tool and monitor for unintended usage"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. Replacing a general tool with a constrained specialised one fixes selection and capability at the same time.",
"A request in a description is guidance with no enforcement.",
"A deny-list in a <em>description</em> enforces nothing, and deny-lists are inherently incomplete even when enforced properly.",
"Monitoring detects the problem repeatedly without preventing it."],
e:"The blueprint names this pattern directly: replace general tools with constrained alternatives, giving <code>fetch_url</code> &rarr; <code>load_document</code> as the example. The win is twofold. Selection improves because a narrow tool with a clear contract is easier to choose correctly. Security improves because the capability itself is narrowed &mdash; the agent can no longer reach internal APIs at all, rather than being asked not to.",
k:"Prefer narrow, purpose-built tools over general ones. Constraining the contract constrains the capability &mdash; better selection and better security."},

{i:174,d:2,t:"2.1",s:"AAT",diff:3,
q:"A tool's description is thorough, but the agent still calls it in inappropriate situations. Investigation shows the system prompt describes the agent as a &ldquo;data analyst&rdquo;, and the tool is named <code>run_analysis</code>.",
ask:"What is the likely cause?",
a:["The system prompt wording creates an unintended association with the tool's name, biasing selection independently of the description",
"The tool description is being ignored because the system prompt takes precedence",
"The tool name is too short and should be lengthened",
"The agent needs more tools so this one is chosen less often"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. Vocabulary overlap between the persona and the tool name creates a pull toward that tool that the description cannot fully counteract.",
"Descriptions are not overridden by the system prompt; both contribute to selection, which is exactly why the interaction matters.",
"Length is not the issue &mdash; the semantic association is.",
"Adding tools to dilute one tool's attraction is a strange remedy that degrades overall selection reliability."],
e:"The blueprint notes that system prompt wording can create unintended associations with tools. Selection is driven by everything in context, so a persona described as a &ldquo;data analyst&rdquo; primes anything that sounds analytical. Fix it from both ends: rename the tool to describe what it concretely does (<code>compute_cohort_retention</code>), and reword the persona to describe the agent's responsibilities rather than echoing tool vocabulary.",
k:"System prompt vocabulary biases tool selection. Watch for persona wording that echoes tool names; fix from both ends."},

{i:175,d:2,t:"2.1",s:"SDE",diff:2,
q:"A single <code>process_document</code> tool handles classification, extraction and validation depending on a <code>mode</code> parameter. The agent frequently passes the wrong mode.",
ask:"What is the better design?",
a:["Split it into <code>classify_document</code>, <code>extract_fields</code> and <code>validate_extraction</code>, each with its own clear input and output contract",
"Add a more detailed description of what each mode does",
"Default the <code>mode</code> parameter to the most common value",
"Add validation that rejects mismatched mode and input combinations"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. Splitting a multi-mode tool gives each operation a distinct name, description and schema &mdash; all three selection signals become unambiguous.",
"Documenting a confusing interface better does not make it less confusing; the mode parameter still has to be reasoned about on every call.",
"Defaults hide the problem and make the wrong behaviour the silent default for the other two modes.",
"Catching errors after the fact still costs a turn each time and never improves selection."],
e:"The blueprint prescribes splitting general-purpose tools into specialised ones with clear input/output contracts. A mode parameter forces the model to make two decisions in one call &mdash; which tool, then which behaviour &mdash; and the second is invisible to the selection machinery, since all three modes share one name and one description. Separate tools also let you scope permissions differently and give each a schema that only contains its own relevant fields.",
k:"Split multi-mode tools into separate tools. A <code>mode</code> parameter hides a selection decision from the name, description and schema."},

{i:176,d:2,t:"2.1",s:"DPT",diff:2,
q:"You are writing a description for a tool that searches an internal wiki. Which description is strongest?",
ask:"Which is the best description?",
a:["Searches the engineering wiki by full-text query. Use for internal process, runbook and architecture-decision questions. Accepts a query string and optional space filter. Returns up to 10 excerpts with page URLs. Does not search code &mdash; use <code>Grep</code> for that.",
"Searches the wiki.",
"A powerful and flexible search tool for finding information quickly and efficiently across the organisation's knowledge base.",
"Searches the wiki. Takes a query. Returns results. Use when you need wiki information."],
c:0,
w:["Correct. It states purpose, trigger conditions, input format, output shape and an explicit boundary against the nearest alternative.",
"Minimal descriptions are the stated cause of unreliable selection.",
"Adjectives without information. &ldquo;Powerful and flexible&rdquo; helps the model decide nothing, and &ldquo;knowledge base&rdquo; is vaguer than &ldquo;wiki&rdquo;.",
"Slightly better than B but circular &mdash; &ldquo;use when you need wiki information&rdquo; conveys nothing the name did not already."],
e:"A strong tool description answers five questions: what does it do, when should it be chosen, what input does it take and in what format, what does it return, and what is it <em>not</em> for. That last element does the most work in systems with several similar tools, because selection failures are usually near-misses between neighbours rather than wild guesses. Marketing language is worse than useless &mdash; it consumes tokens and adds no discriminating signal.",
k:"Description checklist: what &middot; when &middot; input format &middot; output shape &middot; explicit boundary against neighbours. No adjectives."},

{i:177,d:2,t:"2.1",s:"CSA",diff:3,
q:"Adding detailed descriptions to all twelve of your tools improved selection accuracy from 74% to 89%. The remaining errors are concentrated between two tools with genuinely overlapping functionality.",
ask:"What should you do next?",
a:["Redesign the boundary between those two tools &mdash; merge them, or split them along a different axis so their responsibilities no longer overlap",
"Add another 500 words to each of the two descriptions",
"Accept 89% as the practical ceiling",
"Add few-shot examples covering only those two tools"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. When the functional boundary is genuinely ambiguous, no amount of description can make selection unambiguous &mdash; the design must change.",
"Longer descriptions of an ambiguous distinction restate the ambiguity at greater length and cost.",
"Premature. The remaining errors have an identified, addressable structural cause.",
"A reasonable stopgap, but it teaches the model to navigate a bad boundary rather than removing it."],
e:"There is a ceiling to what descriptions can fix. If two tools genuinely overlap &mdash; both can legitimately serve the same request &mdash; the model's uncertainty reflects reality rather than a description defect. The remedies are structural: merge them into one tool if the distinction does not survive scrutiny, or re-split along an axis that is actually unambiguous (by data source rather than by operation, say, or by permission level). Few-shot is a fine bridge while you do that work.",
k:"Descriptions cannot fix a genuinely ambiguous boundary. Merge the tools or re-split along an unambiguous axis."},

{i:178,d:2,t:"2.1",s:"AAT",diff:2,
q:"A tool accepts a <code>date_range</code> parameter but the description does not say what format it expects. The agent supplies varying formats and roughly one call in six fails validation.",
ask:"Which fix is most effective?",
a:["Document the exact expected format with an example in both the description and the schema, and add a <code>format</code> or pattern constraint",
"Make the tool accept any date format and parse it leniently",
"Add a retry that reformats the date after a validation failure",
"Instruct the agent in the system prompt to always use ISO 8601 dates"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. Stating the format where the model reads it, and constraining it in the schema, removes the guesswork at the source.",
"Lenient parsing is genuinely tempting but introduces ambiguity: <code>03/04/2026</code> has two valid readings and the tool would silently pick one.",
"Burns a turn on one call in six and leaves the underlying ambiguity in place.",
"A global instruction competes with everything else in the system prompt and is easy to overlook for one parameter among many."],
e:"Input format specification belongs in the description and the schema together, because they serve different roles: the description is prose the model reads while deciding how to call, and the schema is the machine-checkable constraint. The blueprint lists input formats among the things a good description must state. Beware lenient parsing as a fix &mdash; for ambiguous formats like <code>MM/DD</code> versus <code>DD/MM</code> it converts a loud failure into a silent wrong answer.",
k:"Specify input formats in the description <em>and</em> constrain them in the schema. Avoid lenient parsing where the format is genuinely ambiguous."},

{i:179,d:2,t:"2.1",s:"MAR",diff:2,
q:"Your research agent has a <code>web_search</code> tool and a <code>verify_fact</code> tool. The <code>verify_fact</code> description says: &ldquo;Verifies a fact.&rdquo; The agent uses <code>web_search</code> for verification instead.",
ask:"What should the description convey?",
a:["That it takes a specific claim and returns a confirmation status with supporting sources, and that it should be preferred over open search for checking a single stated fact",
"That it is faster than <code>web_search</code>",
"That it is the newer of the two tools",
"That the agent should use it whenever it is unsure"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. It states the input contract, the output contract, and the decision rule against the neighbouring tool.",
"Speed is a weak selection signal and does not describe when the tool is appropriate.",
"Recency is irrelevant to selection and will be meaningless to the model.",
"&ldquo;Whenever unsure&rdquo; is vague and over-broad &mdash; it would trigger on situations open search handles better."],
e:"Narrow tools need especially sharp descriptions, because a broad neighbour will otherwise absorb their traffic: <code>web_search</code> can technically verify a fact, so the model needs a reason to prefer the specialised tool. State the input contract (a specific claim, not a topic), the output contract (confirmed / contradicted / not found, with sources), and the explicit preference rule. This is the same design that made the earlier <code>verify_fact</code> least-privilege answer work.",
k:"A narrow tool needs an explicit preference rule against its broad neighbour, or the broad tool absorbs its traffic."},

{i:180,d:2,t:"2.1",s:"AAT",diff:3,
q:"You add a new tool and selection accuracy for three <em>existing</em> tools drops.",
ask:"What is the most likely explanation?",
a:["The new tool's description overlaps semantically with the existing three, pulling calls that should have gone to them",
"Adding tools always reduces accuracy proportionally and nothing can be done",
"The existing tools' descriptions were silently truncated to make room",
"The new tool has a bug that corrupts the others"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. Selection is comparative, so a new tool changes the decision landscape for its semantic neighbours.",
"Tool count does degrade selection, but a targeted drop in exactly three tools points at overlap, not at a general effect.",
"Descriptions are not silently truncated.",
"Tool definitions are independent metadata; one cannot corrupt another."],
e:"Selection is a comparison, not an independent judgement per tool, so every addition perturbs its neighbourhood. When accuracy drops for a specific set after an addition, compare the new description against those specific ones and look for shared vocabulary and overlapping trigger conditions. The fix is to sharpen boundaries on both sides &mdash; the new tool states what it is not for, and the existing ones state where they still apply.",
k:"Selection is comparative. A new tool perturbs its semantic neighbours; sharpen boundaries on both sides after every addition."},

{i:181,d:2,t:"2.2",s:"CSA",diff:2,
q:"Your MCP tool returns <code>{\"isError\": true, \"content\": \"Operation failed\"}</code> for every failure &mdash; timeouts, invalid inputs, permission denials and policy violations alike. The agent's recovery behaviour is inconsistent and often wrong.",
ask:"What should the error response include?",
a:["An <code>errorCategory</code> (transient / validation / permission / business), an <code>isRetryable</code> flag, and a human-readable message describing what failed",
"A longer stack trace so the agent can diagnose the cause itself",
"A numeric error code the agent can look up in the system prompt",
"The word FAILED in capitals so the agent recognises it clearly"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. Category plus retryability plus a readable message is exactly the structured metadata the blueprint prescribes.",
"Stack traces are internal detail, expensive in tokens, and give the model no clear signal about what to do next.",
"A code plus a lookup table pushes interpretation into the prompt and breaks whenever the table drifts from the implementation.",
"Emphasis is not information &mdash; the agent still cannot tell a timeout from a policy violation."],
e:"Generic errors prevent correct recovery decisions, and the four categories demand four different responses. <strong>Transient</strong> (timeout, rate limit): retry, ideally with backoff. <strong>Validation</strong> (bad input): fix the arguments, do not retry unchanged. <strong>Permission</strong>: do not retry, escalate or route differently. <strong>Business rule</strong>: never retryable &mdash; explain to the user and offer the alternative path. The <code>isRetryable</code> flag makes the decision explicit rather than inferred.",
k:"MCP error contract: <code>errorCategory</code> + <code>isRetryable</code> + actionable message. Four categories, four different recoveries."},

{i:182,d:2,t:"2.2",s:"CSA",diff:3,
q:"A refund is rejected because the order is outside the return window. The tool returns <code>{\"isError\": true, \"errorCategory\": \"business\", \"isRetryable\": false, \"message\": \"Order 8823 was delivered 47 days ago; the standard return window is 30 days.\"}</code>",
ask:"Why is this a good error response?",
a:["It names the rule, gives the specific facts, and marks itself non-retryable so the agent explains the situation rather than retrying",
"It is short enough to save tokens",
"It hides internal details from the customer",
"It allows the agent to retry with different parameters"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. Category, retryability and a specific, explainable message together produce a graceful, informative outcome.",
"Brevity is incidental; the value is in the specificity and the structured flags.",
"It actually exposes the relevant business facts deliberately &mdash; that is what makes the agent's explanation useful.",
"The opposite: <code>isRetryable: false</code> tells the agent that no retry will succeed, which is the point."],
e:"Contrast this with &ldquo;Operation failed&rdquo;. The agent can now tell the customer exactly why, cite the dates, and offer the correct next step &mdash; a goodwill exception request routed to a human, say &mdash; rather than retrying blindly and then producing a vague apology. Note the blueprint's phrasing: use <code>retryable: false</code> for business-rule violations with clear user-facing explanations. The message is written to be repeated to a customer.",
k:"Business-rule errors: non-retryable, with the rule named and the specific facts included so the agent can explain it to the user."},

{i:183,d:2,t:"2.2",s:"MAR",diff:3,
q:"A search tool returns zero results. Your implementation returns <code>{\"isError\": true, \"message\": \"No results\"}</code>. The agent treats this as a failure and retries several times.",
ask:"What is wrong?",
a:["An empty result set is a valid successful outcome, not an error, and conflating the two makes retries look worthwhile",
"The error message is too short",
"The tool should return an error code instead of a message",
"The agent should be instructed never to retry search"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. Distinguishing access failures from valid empty results is called out explicitly in the blueprint.",
"Length is not the issue; the classification is.",
"A code has the same problem &mdash; it still classifies a valid outcome as a failure.",
"Too blunt: retrying a genuine timeout is correct behaviour, and this rule would suppress that too."],
e:"&ldquo;I searched and found nothing&rdquo; and &ldquo;I could not search&rdquo; demand opposite responses: the first is a finding to report, the second is a failure to recover from. Return success with an empty result array and, ideally, an echo of the query so the agent can see what was searched. Reserve <code>isError</code> for cases where the operation itself could not be performed. Getting this wrong causes retry storms on one side and silently missing gaps on the other.",
k:"Empty result = success with an empty set. Access failure = <code>isError</code>. Never conflate &ldquo;found nothing&rdquo; with &ldquo;could not look&rdquo;."},

{i:184,d:2,t:"2.2",s:"AAT",diff:2,
q:"A subagent's tool hits a transient network timeout. The subagent immediately propagates the error to the coordinator without attempting anything.",
ask:"What would better design look like?",
a:["The subagent retries transient failures locally within a bounded budget, and propagates to the coordinator only what it cannot resolve, including partial results",
"The subagent always propagates every error immediately for central handling",
"The subagent suppresses the error and returns whatever it has as a success",
"The coordinator retries every failure regardless of category"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. Handle errors at the lowest level capable of resolving them; escalate only the genuinely unresolvable, with context.",
"Central handling of routine transient faults floods the coordinator with work it has no better information to handle.",
"Masking failure as success is the most dangerous option &mdash; the gap becomes invisible.",
"Category-blind retry at any level wastes attempts on deterministic failures."],
e:"The blueprint states it directly: do local recovery inside subagents for transient failures, and propagate only errors they cannot resolve. The division of labour is clean &mdash; the subagent knows the operation and can retry it cheaply; the coordinator knows the workflow and can re-route, re-scope or annotate. What crosses the boundary should be an unresolvable problem with enough context to act on: what failed, why, what was salvaged, what alternatives exist.",
k:"Retry transient failures locally in the subagent. Escalate only the unresolvable, carrying partial results and alternatives."},

{i:185,d:2,t:"2.2",s:"SDE",diff:2,
q:"An extraction tool receives a malformed date and returns <code>{\"isError\": true, \"errorCategory\": \"transient\", \"isRetryable\": true, \"message\": \"Could not parse input\"}</code>. The agent retries the identical call four times.",
ask:"What is the defect?",
a:["A validation error has been miscategorised as transient and marked retryable, so the agent retries something that can never succeed",
"The agent should have a lower retry limit",
"The message should include the malformed value",
"The tool should have parsed the date leniently"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. The category and retryability flags are wrong, so the agent's otherwise-sensible retry logic produces a loop.",
"Lowering the limit reduces the waste without fixing the miscategorisation that causes it.",
"Including the value would genuinely improve the message &mdash; but the retry loop is caused by the wrong flags, not by the message.",
"Lenient parsing introduces silent misinterpretation of ambiguous formats, which is worse than a clear rejection."],
e:"Misclassifying an error is as harmful as not classifying it, because the caller acts on your classification. A validation error should be <code>errorCategory: \"validation\"</code> with <code>isRetryable: false</code> for the identical call &mdash; and the message should say precisely what was wrong and what the expected format is, so the agent can retry with <em>corrected</em> arguments. That distinction matters: retry-with-correction is valid for validation errors; retry-unchanged never is.",
k:"Validation errors are not retryable unchanged, but are retryable <em>corrected</em>. State the expected format so the agent can fix its arguments."},

{i:186,d:2,t:"2.2",s:"AAT",diff:3,
q:"Which error response best supports intelligent coordinator recovery when a research subagent times out?",
ask:"Which response is best?",
a:["Structured context: failure type, the query attempted, partial results already gathered, and suggested alternatives",
"Retry internally with exponential backoff, then return a generic &ldquo;search unavailable&rdquo; status",
"Catch the timeout and return an empty result set marked as success",
"Propagate the raw timeout exception to a top-level handler that terminates the workflow"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. This gives the coordinator what it needs to choose between retrying with a modified query, trying another route, or proceeding with annotated partial coverage.",
"Backoff is fine, but collapsing the outcome into a generic status throws away the context the coordinator needs.",
"Masking failure as success means the gap becomes invisible and appears in the final report as a silent omission.",
"Terminating an entire workflow because one subagent timed out discards everything that succeeded."],
e:"This mirrors a published sample question. The four options map exactly onto the blueprint's error-handling taxonomy: the correct structured-context answer, and the three named anti-patterns &mdash; generic status hiding context, silent suppression masking failure as success, and whole-workflow abort. Learn the shape of the distractors as well as the answer, because this question appears in several disguises.",
k:"Structured failure context: type, what was attempted, partial results, alternatives. Anti-patterns: generic status, silent suppression, whole-workflow abort."},

{i:187,d:2,t:"2.2",s:"CSA",diff:2,
q:"A tool returns an error whose message is written for developers: <code>NullPointerException at OrderService.java:412</code>. The agent relays this to the customer.",
ask:"What are the two problems?",
a:["The message leaks internal implementation detail to a customer, and it gives the agent no information about what to do next",
"The message is too short and should include the full stack trace",
"The exception type is wrong for this situation",
"The agent should never relay tool messages to customers under any circumstances"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. It is both an information-disclosure problem and an actionability problem.",
"A full stack trace makes the disclosure worse and is still unactionable.",
"The specific exception type is a symptom; the error contract is the defect.",
"Too absolute &mdash; well-written business-rule messages are explicitly designed to be explained to customers."],
e:"Error messages have two audiences and they need different content. The model needs category, retryability and enough detail to choose a recovery. The customer needs a plain-language explanation and a next step. Internal diagnostics belong in your logs, keyed by a correlation id you can include in the response. A well-designed error response separates these concerns rather than dumping a stack trace into a context that will be paraphrased to a user.",
k:"Separate audiences: category and retryability for the model, plain language for the user, stack traces for your logs behind a correlation id."},

{i:188,d:2,t:"2.2",s:"AAT",diff:2,
q:"Which situation should be marked <code>isRetryable: true</code>?",
ask:"Which is retryable?",
a:["A 503 from a downstream service under momentary load",
"A refund amount that exceeds the policy limit",
"A malformed argument that fails schema validation",
"An unauthorised caller without the required scope"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. Transient infrastructure failures are the canonical retryable case.",
"A business-rule violation is deterministic &mdash; the same call will be rejected every time.",
"Retrying the identical malformed argument fails identically; only a corrected call could succeed.",
"Permissions do not change between two calls a second apart; this needs escalation or a different route."],
e:"The classification test is simple: would the identical call, made again shortly, plausibly succeed? Only transient infrastructure conditions qualify &mdash; timeouts, rate limits, 502/503, transient connection resets. Everything deterministic (business rules, validation, permissions) is not retryable as-is. Note the nuance for validation: the identical call is not retryable, but a corrected call is, which is why the message must state the expected format.",
k:"Retryable test: would the identical call succeed shortly? Only transient infrastructure. Business, validation and permission errors are deterministic."},

{i:189,d:2,t:"2.2",s:"MAR",diff:3,
q:"A document-analysis subagent fails on PDFs in three distinct ways: some are corrupted, some are password-protected, and the parser occasionally hangs on very large files. Today any exception terminates the subagent and returns an error, forcing the coordinator to handle routine problems.",
ask:"Which architectural improvement is most effective?",
a:["Implement local recovery in the subagent for the failures it can handle, and escalate only unresolvable errors with the steps attempted and any partial results",
"Have the coordinator pre-validate every document before dispatch, rejecting any that might fail",
"Configure the subagent to always return partial results with a success status, embedding error detail in metadata",
"Create a dedicated error-handling agent that watches a shared failure queue and issues restart commands to subagents"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. Handle errors at the lowest level capable of resolving them; escalate the rest with full context and partial progress.",
"Pre-validation duplicates the parser's work in the coordinator and cannot reliably predict a parser hang.",
"Reporting failure as success is the silent-suppression anti-pattern &mdash; the coordinator loses the ability to react at all.",
"A separate error agent with direct command channels to subagents breaks hub-and-spoke and adds a component with no better information."],
e:"This mirrors a published sample question. The three failure modes have different local remedies &mdash; skip corrupted sections and report reduced coverage, mark password-protected files as needing credentials, apply a timeout and process large files in chunks &mdash; and the subagent is best placed to apply them. What reaches the coordinator should be genuinely unresolvable, with the attempted steps and any partial results included.",
k:"Handle errors at the lowest capable level. Escalate only the unresolvable, with attempted steps and partial results attached."},

{i:190,d:2,t:"2.2",s:"AAT",diff:2,
q:"Your team proposes returning HTTP-style numeric status codes from MCP tools instead of structured error objects.",
ask:"What is the main drawback?",
a:["A bare code carries no context about what was attempted or what to do next, so the model must infer recovery from a number",
"Numeric codes are not supported by the MCP specification",
"Numeric codes always consume more tokens than text",
"Models cannot process numbers reliably"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. Codes compress away exactly the context that makes intelligent recovery possible.",
"You can put whatever you like in a tool result; the objection is about usefulness, not support.",
"Codes are shorter, which is precisely why they are tempting and why they lose information.",
"Models handle numbers fine; the problem is that a number is not a plan."],
e:"HTTP codes work between systems that share a specification. A language model deciding what to do next needs the semantics, not the shorthand: what operation failed, on what input, why, whether retrying helps, and what the alternative is. A structured object with <code>errorCategory</code>, <code>isRetryable</code> and a specific message carries all of that. If you want codes for your own metrics, include one <em>alongside</em> the structured fields rather than instead of them.",
k:"Structured error objects beat bare codes. The model needs semantics and a recovery signal, not a number to look up."},

{i:191,d:2,t:"2.2",s:"SDE",diff:2,
q:"A validation error message reads: &ldquo;Invalid input.&rdquo; You want the agent to be able to correct and retry successfully.",
ask:"What must the message add?",
a:["Which field was invalid, what value was received, and what format or value set is expected",
"An apology and a request to try again",
"The name of the validation library used",
"A suggestion to contact support"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. Field, received value and expectation is exactly what a corrected retry needs.",
"Politeness carries no information and does not enable correction.",
"Implementation detail that is irrelevant to the caller.",
"Escalates a problem the agent could fix itself in one turn."],
e:"Validation errors are the one non-retryable category that becomes retryable once corrected, which makes their message content unusually valuable. Name the field, echo the offending value, state the expectation (&ldquo;expected ISO 8601 date, for example 2026-03-04; received 03/04/2026&rdquo;), and where several fields failed, list them all so one retry fixes everything rather than discovering errors one at a time.",
k:"Validation messages: field + received value + expected format. List every failing field so one corrected retry fixes them all."},

{i:192,d:2,t:"2.2",s:"AAT",diff:3,
q:"Your tool returns partial results alongside an error when it can complete some of the requested work but not all of it.",
ask:"What must the response make unambiguous?",
a:["Exactly which parts succeeded, which failed and why, so downstream consumers do not treat partial data as complete",
"That the overall operation should be considered successful",
"That the operation should be retried in full",
"Nothing &mdash; partial results should never be returned"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. Partial success is only useful when the boundary between the two halves is explicit and machine-readable.",
"Reporting partial as fully successful is the silent-suppression anti-pattern.",
"Full retry discards the work that succeeded and may fail again on the same portion.",
"Too absolute &mdash; partial results are explicitly valuable, provided the gaps are annotated."],
e:"Partial success is common and useful in agentic systems &mdash; 8 of 10 documents parsed, 3 of 4 sources reachable &mdash; but only if the response says which is which. Mark the successful items, list the failures with their reasons and retryability, and make the aggregate status explicitly &ldquo;partial&rdquo; rather than success or failure. This is what feeds coverage annotation in the final output, so a reader can see that the music section rests on two sources instead of five.",
k:"Partial results need explicit boundaries: what succeeded, what failed, why, and an aggregate status of &ldquo;partial&rdquo; &mdash; never success."},

{i:193,d:2,t:"2.2",s:"CSA",diff:2,
q:"Your agent receives a permission error from <code>process_refund</code> because the customer's account is flagged for manual review.",
ask:"What should the agent do?",
a:["Explain the situation and route to escalation, since permission errors are not resolvable by retrying or rephrasing",
"Retry with a smaller refund amount to see if that is permitted",
"Try a different tool that might accomplish the same thing",
"Tell the customer the system is temporarily unavailable"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. A permission condition is deterministic and outside the agent's authority, which makes it an escalation.",
"Probing a permission boundary by varying arguments is both futile and a poor pattern to encourage.",
"Looking for a tool that bypasses a deliberate control is exactly the behaviour permission errors exist to prevent.",
"Misrepresents a deliberate account flag as an outage, which misleads the customer and hides the real situation."],
e:"Permission errors carry information beyond &ldquo;it did not work&rdquo;: something about this account or this actor requires human involvement. The right response is to explain what is happening in plain terms and hand off with a structured escalation payload including the flag reason. Note the shape of the wrong answers &mdash; two of them attempt to work around the control, and one hides it behind a false explanation.",
k:"Permission errors &rarr; explain and escalate. Never probe the boundary, never route around the control, never misreport it as an outage."},

{i:194,d:2,t:"2.2",s:"AAT",diff:2,
q:"You are designing error responses for a tool used by both an agent and a traditional service.",
ask:"Which approach serves both well?",
a:["Return a structured object with machine-readable category and retryability fields plus a human-readable message",
"Return prose only, since the agent reads text",
"Return codes only, since the service parses codes",
"Return different formats depending on the caller"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. Structured fields serve programmatic consumers, the message serves the model and the eventual user, and one contract serves both.",
"Prose forces the service to parse natural language, which is brittle.",
"Codes alone starve the agent of the context it needs to choose a recovery.",
"Divergent formats double the surface area and guarantee they will drift apart."],
e:"A well-designed error object serves both audiences at once: <code>errorCategory</code> and <code>isRetryable</code> are stable, machine-readable fields a service can branch on, while <code>message</code> is the human-readable explanation the model reasons over and may relay to a user. One contract, one implementation, no drift. Add a correlation id so an operator can find the full internal detail in your logs without it being in the response.",
k:"One error contract serves both: structured fields for machines, a clear message for the model and the user, a correlation id for your logs."},

{i:195,d:2,t:"2.3",s:"CSA",diff:3,
q:"Your support agent has 18 tools spanning billing, shipping, technical diagnostics and account management. Tool-selection accuracy is 68%.",
ask:"What is the most effective structural change?",
a:["Split into specialised agents, each holding only the 4&ndash;6 tools relevant to its role, coordinated by a router",
"Improve all 18 tool descriptions and keep them on one agent",
"Reorder the tool list so the most-used tools appear first",
"Give the agent more few-shot examples of correct selection"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. The blueprint contrasts 18 tools with a focused 4&ndash;5 precisely because toolset size degrades selection; specialisation is the structural fix.",
"Worth doing regardless, but at 18 tools the choice space itself is the limiting factor, so descriptions alone will not get you there.",
"Ordering is not a documented selection lever and does not address the size of the choice space.",
"Examples help at the margin and cost tokens on every request; they do not fix an oversized toolset."],
e:"The blueprint states that too many tools per agent &mdash; giving 18 versus 4&ndash;5 as the illustration &mdash; reduces tool-selection reliability, and that agents holding tools outside their specialisation tend to misuse them. The structural remedy is scoped access: each agent gets its role-relevant tools plus a small set of cross-role utilities. Sharpening descriptions remains worthwhile within each agent, but it is a complement, not a substitute.",
k:"Roughly 4&ndash;6 role-relevant tools per agent. Beyond that, split into specialists rather than polishing descriptions."},

{i:196,d:2,t:"2.3",s:"AAT",diff:2,
q:"You need to guarantee that a model call produces a tool invocation rather than a text answer, and any of three extraction schemas would be acceptable.",
ask:"Which <code>tool_choice</code> setting applies?",
a:["<code>{\"type\": \"any\"}</code>",
"<code>{\"type\": \"auto\"}</code>",
"<code>{\"type\": \"tool\", \"name\": \"extract\"}</code>",
"<code>{\"type\": \"none\"}</code>"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. <code>any</code> forces some tool call while leaving the model free to pick among the three.",
"<code>auto</code> permits a plain text response, which is the outcome you need to exclude.",
"Forcing one specific tool removes the model's ability to choose among the three valid schemas.",
"<code>none</code> forbids tool calls entirely &mdash; the opposite of the requirement."],
e:"Match the setting to the requirement. <code>auto</code>: model decides between text and tools &mdash; the default for conversational loops. <code>any</code>: must call some tool, model picks which &mdash; guarantees structured output when several schemas are valid. <code>{\"type\": \"tool\", \"name\": ...}</code>: must call that specific tool &mdash; for single-shot extraction against one known schema. <code>none</code>: no tools this turn. Remember that <code>any</code> in a multi-turn loop prevents the loop from ever terminating naturally.",
k:"Several valid schemas, must be structured &rarr; <code>any</code>. One known schema &rarr; forced tool. Conversational &rarr; <code>auto</code>."},

{i:197,d:2,t:"2.3",s:"SDE",diff:2,
q:"An extraction job must always call <code>extract_invoice_fields</code> and never any other tool, on every document.",
ask:"Which configuration is correct?",
a:["<code>tool_choice: {\"type\": \"tool\", \"name\": \"extract_invoice_fields\"}</code>",
"<code>tool_choice: {\"type\": \"any\"}</code> with only that tool defined",
"<code>tool_choice: {\"type\": \"auto\"}</code> with a strong prompt instruction",
"<code>tool_choice: {\"type\": \"none\"}</code> with the schema in the prompt"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. Forcing the named tool is the direct, explicit expression of the requirement.",
"Functionally equivalent when exactly one tool is defined, but it is fragile &mdash; adding a second tool later silently changes the behaviour.",
"<code>auto</code> permits a text response no matter how the prompt is worded.",
"<code>none</code> forbids tool use, so no structured tool output is possible."],
e:"Forcing a specific tool is the standard single-shot structured-extraction pattern: define one tool whose <code>input_schema</code> is your target schema, force it, and read the extracted object from the <code>tool_use</code> block's <code>input</code>. Note why B is a trap even though it works today &mdash; it depends on an invariant (exactly one tool defined) that a future change can break silently. Express the constraint explicitly.",
k:"Single-shot extraction: define the schema as a tool, force it by name, read the object from <code>tool_use.input</code>. Be explicit, not incidentally correct."},

{i:198,d:2,t:"2.3",s:"MAR",diff:2,
q:"A synthesis agent needs to check simple facts but must not perform open-ended web research.",
ask:"Which tool allocation is correct?",
a:["Give it a narrow <code>verify_fact</code> tool only, and route complex research through the coordinator to the search agent",
"Give it the full <code>web_search</code> toolset so it can handle anything",
"Give it no tools and require it to flag every uncertain claim for the coordinator",
"Give it <code>web_search</code> but instruct it in the prompt to use it only for simple checks"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. Least privilege sized to the actual need, with the escalation path preserved for the harder cases.",
"Grants capability far beyond the role, degrading both selection reliability and separation of concerns.",
"Too restrictive &mdash; it recreates the round-trip overhead that giving a narrow tool was meant to remove.",
"Relies on a prompt to fence off a capability you granted. Guidance, not enforcement."],
e:"Scoped tool access means giving each agent role-relevant tools plus a limited set of cross-role utilities. The narrow <code>verify_fact</code> tool covers the common case within the agent's specialisation; anything requiring genuine investigation goes back through the coordinator to the agent that owns it. Note the recurring wrong answer in D: granting a broad tool and restricting it by prompt is the pattern the exam consistently marks insufficient.",
k:"Scope tools to the role. Narrow tool for the common case, coordinator route for the tail. Never grant broad and restrict by prompt."},

{i:199,d:2,t:"2.3",s:"AAT",diff:3,
q:"You are told that a particular agent needs 12 tools because its role genuinely spans that much surface area.",
ask:"What should you examine first?",
a:["Whether the role is actually two or three roles that should be separate agents",
"Whether the model supports 12 tools",
"Whether the tools can be reordered to improve selection",
"Whether the tool descriptions can be shortened to save tokens"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. A role needing 12 tools is usually a signal that the role is under-decomposed.",
"Twelve tools is well within any limit; capability is not the constraint.",
"Ordering is not a meaningful selection lever.",
"Shortening descriptions makes selection worse, not better."],
e:"Treat tool count as a design smell rather than a hard limit. When a role legitimately needs a dozen tools, look for the seam: billing operations and technical diagnostics are usually separate specialisations that happen to share a queue. Splitting improves selection reliability, allows different permission scopes, and lets each agent have a focused system prompt. Where a genuinely broad role resists splitting, the fallback is narrow constrained tools plus delegation for the tail.",
k:"A role needing many tools is usually under-decomposed. Look for the seam before accepting a large toolset."},

{i:200,d:2,t:"2.3",s:"AAT",diff:2,
q:"Your agent sometimes answers from its own knowledge instead of calling the tool that would give the authoritative answer.",
ask:"Which mechanism most directly prevents this on a given turn?",
a:["Set <code>tool_choice</code> to <code>any</code> for that turn so a tool call is required",
"Add an instruction saying &ldquo;always use tools, never answer from memory&rdquo;",
"Remove the model's ability to produce text output",
"Lower the temperature so the model is less creative"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. <code>any</code> structurally forbids a text-only turn, which is the guarantee being asked for.",
"A prompt instruction is guidance; it will hold most of the time and fail exactly when the model is confident.",
"There is no such setting, and you generally need text output for the final answer.",
"Temperature does not govern whether the model chooses to call a tool."],
e:"This is the constructive use of <code>any</code>: when a turn must be grounded in a tool call rather than in model priors, force it. The important caveat is scope &mdash; apply it to the specific turn that needs grounding, not to the whole loop, because a loop permanently under <code>any</code> can never emit a final answer and will run until the iteration cap. Set it per request as the workflow requires.",
k:"Force grounding with <code>any</code> on the specific turn that needs it. Never leave <code>any</code> set for a whole conversational loop."},

{i:201,d:2,t:"2.3",s:"CSA",diff:3,
q:"You have split into specialised agents. The billing agent occasionally needs to look up an order, which belongs to the shipping agent's toolset.",
ask:"What is the best resolution?",
a:["Give the billing agent a narrow read-only order-lookup tool as a cross-role utility, keeping shipping operations with the shipping agent",
"Give the billing agent the shipping agent's full toolset",
"Route every billing conversation through the shipping agent first",
"Merge billing and shipping into one agent"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. Scoped access is explicitly role-relevant tools <em>plus a limited set of cross-role utilities</em>, which is exactly this case.",
"Grants write capability over shipping operations that billing has no business performing.",
"Adds a mandatory hop to every conversation to serve an occasional need.",
"Discards the specialisation to solve a narrow overlap, and recreates the large-toolset problem."],
e:"Specialisation does not mean airtight isolation. The blueprint's phrasing allows role-relevant tools plus a limited set of cross-role utilities, and read-only lookups are the classic example. The discipline is in the constraint: a read-only <code>lookup_order</code> is a utility; <code>update_shipment</code> and <code>issue_replacement</code> are shipping operations that belong to the shipping agent and stay there.",
k:"Cross-role utilities are allowed and should be read-only and narrow. Write operations stay with the agent that owns the domain."},

{i:202,d:2,t:"2.3",s:"AAT",diff:2,
q:"Which statement about <code>tool_choice: \"auto\"</code> is correct?",
ask:"Which is true?",
a:["The model may respond with text or with one or more tool calls, and it is the appropriate default for conversational agent loops",
"The model must call exactly one tool per turn",
"The model will call every available tool at least once",
"The model cannot call tools; it may only respond with text"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. <code>auto</code> leaves the decision to the model, which is what allows a loop to terminate with <code>end_turn</code>.",
"That describes <code>any</code>, and even then the model may emit several parallel calls.",
"Nothing forces exhaustive tool use.",
"That describes <code>none</code>."],
e:"<code>auto</code> is the default and the right setting for any loop that must be able to finish, because it permits the text-only turn that yields <code>stop_reason: \"end_turn\"</code>. It also permits parallel tool use &mdash; several <code>tool_use</code> blocks in one assistant message when the calls are independent. The related option <code>disable_parallel_tool_use</code> forces one call per turn when your executor cannot handle concurrency.",
k:"<code>auto</code> = model chooses text or tools, and may emit several parallel calls. It is what lets an agent loop terminate."},

{i:203,d:2,t:"2.4",s:"DPT",diff:2,
q:"Your team wants a GitHub MCP server available to everyone who clones the repository, using each developer's own token.",
ask:"How should this be configured?",
a:["Define the server in the project's <code>.mcp.json</code>, referencing the token via environment variable substitution such as <code>${GITHUB_TOKEN}</code>",
"Define it in each developer's <code>~/.claude.json</code> with their token inline",
"Define it in <code>.mcp.json</code> with a shared team token committed to the repository",
"Define it in CLAUDE.md so the configuration is documented for the team"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. Project scope shares the configuration; environment variable substitution keeps the secret out of version control.",
"Works, but every developer must configure it independently and the team gets no shared definition.",
"Committing a credential to the repository is a security incident and defeats per-developer attribution.",
"CLAUDE.md carries instructions and conventions, not MCP server definitions."],
e:"Two facts the exam tests directly. <strong>Scope</strong>: project <code>.mcp.json</code> is version-controlled and shared with the team; user <code>~/.claude.json</code> is personal and suits experiments and personal servers. <strong>Secrets</strong>: <code>.mcp.json</code> supports environment variable substitution, so <code>${GITHUB_TOKEN}</code> lets the shared definition resolve to each developer's own credential without any token entering the repository.",
k:"Team MCP servers &rarr; project <code>.mcp.json</code> with <code>${ENV_VAR}</code> substitution. Personal or experimental &rarr; <code>~/.claude.json</code>."},

{i:204,d:2,t:"2.4",s:"DPT",diff:2,
q:"A developer wants to try an experimental MCP server without affecting anyone else on the team.",
ask:"Where should it be configured?",
a:["In their user-level <code>~/.claude.json</code>",
"In the project's <code>.mcp.json</code>, with a comment saying it is experimental",
"In the project's CLAUDE.md under an &ldquo;experimental&rdquo; heading",
"In a feature branch of <code>.mcp.json</code> that is never merged"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. User scope is exactly for personal and experimental servers.",
"Project scope means the whole team gets it, comment or not, which is the outcome to avoid.",
"CLAUDE.md does not configure MCP servers.",
"An unmerged branch is an awkward workaround that breaks the moment they need to work on another branch."],
e:"The scope decision follows from who should be affected. Project <code>.mcp.json</code> is version-controlled and reaches everyone who clones the repository &mdash; correct for servers the team depends on. User <code>~/.claude.json</code> affects only that developer &mdash; correct for experiments, personal tooling and anything not yet ready for the team. Promote from user to project scope once the server proves itself.",
k:"Scope by blast radius: project <code>.mcp.json</code> for team dependencies, user <code>~/.claude.json</code> for personal and experimental servers."},

{i:205,d:2,t:"2.4",s:"DPT",diff:3,
q:"You connect three MCP servers. A developer asks which tools are available and whether they need to activate a server before using its tools.",
ask:"What is the correct answer?",
a:["Tools from all connected MCP servers are discovered on connection and are available simultaneously &mdash; no activation step is needed",
"Only one MCP server can be active at a time; you switch between them",
"Tools must be individually enabled in <code>.mcp.json</code> before use",
"MCP servers must be activated with a slash command at the start of each session"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. Connection is discovery: every connected server's tools join the available set together.",
"There is no exclusivity between servers.",
"Configuring the server makes its tools available; there is no separate per-tool enablement step.",
"No such activation command is required."],
e:"A direct configuration fact, and it carries a design consequence the exam cares about: because every connected server's tools are available at once, connecting many servers inflates the toolset and degrades selection reliability. That is why the scope decision matters &mdash; put only genuinely shared servers in project <code>.mcp.json</code>, and consider which agents need which tools rather than connecting everything everywhere.",
k:"All connected MCP servers' tools are available simultaneously on connection. More servers = a bigger toolset = worse selection."},

{i:206,d:2,t:"2.4",s:"DPT",diff:3,
q:"Your agent repeatedly runs exploratory tool calls to work out which database tables exist and what the task statuses mean, before doing any real work.",
ask:"Which MCP capability best addresses this?",
a:["MCP resources, exposing schemas and status catalogues as readable content the agent can consult directly",
"More tools, one per table, so the agent can query each directly",
"A larger context window so the exploration fits comfortably",
"A prompt instruction listing the tables and statuses"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. Resources are content catalogues &mdash; schemas, task summaries, reference data &mdash; that remove the need for exploratory tool calls.",
"One tool per table explodes the toolset and degrades selection dramatically.",
"Accommodates the waste rather than eliminating it, and the exploration still costs turns.",
"Workable for a small static list, but it bloats the system prompt and goes stale silently as the schema evolves."],
e:"MCP distinguishes tools (actions the model invokes) from resources (content the model can read). The blueprint highlights resources as content catalogues &mdash; task summaries, database schemas &mdash; that reduce exploratory tool calls. The advantage over stuffing the same information into a prompt is that resources are served by the MCP server, so they stay current as the underlying system changes, and they are read on demand rather than occupying context on every request.",
k:"MCP tools = actions. MCP resources = readable content catalogues (schemas, reference data) that eliminate exploratory tool calls."},

{i:207,d:2,t:"2.4",s:"DPT",diff:2,
q:"Your team needs standard Jira integration. A developer proposes writing a custom MCP server for it.",
ask:"What should you consider first?",
a:["Whether a maintained community MCP server already covers the integration, since building and maintaining your own is a long-term cost",
"Whether the custom server can be written in the same language as the main application",
"Whether a custom server would be faster than a community one",
"Whether Jira's API supports MCP natively"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. The blueprint explicitly favours community MCP servers over custom ones for standard integrations.",
"Language choice is irrelevant &mdash; MCP servers communicate over a protocol, not by linking.",
"Marginal performance differences rarely justify owning an integration forever.",
"MCP is a layer you build over an API; native support is not a prerequisite."],
e:"For standard integrations &mdash; GitHub, Jira, Slack, Postgres &mdash; prefer an existing community MCP server. You inherit maintenance, API-change tracking and community testing rather than owning them. Build custom when the integration is genuinely proprietary, when you need to expose a narrower or safer surface than a general server offers, or when you must enforce internal policy at the tool boundary.",
k:"Prefer community MCP servers for standard integrations. Build custom for proprietary systems or when you need a deliberately narrower surface."},

{i:208,d:2,t:"2.4",s:"AAT",diff:2,
q:"A secret is currently hardcoded in a committed <code>.mcp.json</code>.",
ask:"What is the correct remediation?",
a:["Replace the literal with an environment variable reference, rotate the exposed credential, and purge it from history",
"Move the <code>.mcp.json</code> file into <code>.gitignore</code>",
"Move the server definition to <code>~/.claude.json</code> for every developer",
"Encrypt the <code>.mcp.json</code> file in the repository"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. All three steps are needed: fix the mechanism, rotate the compromised secret, and remove it from history where it remains readable.",
"Ignoring the file forward does nothing about the secret already in history, and it removes the shared configuration the team needs.",
"Loses the shared definition and still leaves the committed secret exposed.",
"Adds a key-distribution problem, and the plaintext remains in history regardless."],
e:"Note that the correct answer has three parts, and incomplete remediation is the trap: once a secret is committed it is compromised, so rotation is mandatory even after you fix the reference and rewrite history. The forward-looking mechanism is environment variable substitution in <code>.mcp.json</code>, which keeps the definition shared and version-controlled while the credential stays in each developer's environment or your secret manager.",
k:"Committed secret = compromised. Replace with <code>${ENV_VAR}</code>, rotate the credential, purge from history. All three."},

{i:209,d:2,t:"2.4",s:"DPT",diff:3,
q:"After connecting a fourth MCP server, an agent that previously performed well starts choosing inappropriate tools.",
ask:"What is the most likely cause and the appropriate response?",
a:["The combined toolset has grown past the point of reliable selection; scope which agents get which servers rather than connecting everything everywhere",
"The fourth server is misconfigured and should be removed",
"The agent's context window is now too small",
"MCP servers conflict when more than three are connected"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. All connected servers' tools are available at once, so each connection enlarges the choice space for every agent.",
"Possible but not the first hypothesis &mdash; the symptom is degraded selection across the board, not failures from one server.",
"Tool definitions consume context but the symptom described is selection quality, not truncation.",
"There is no three-server limit; the effect is a gradual quality curve."],
e:"This links two facts: all connected MCP servers' tools are simultaneously available, and selection reliability degrades as the toolset grows. Together they mean MCP connections are not free &mdash; each one taxes every agent that can see it. The remedy is scoping: give specialised agents only the servers their role needs, keep experimental servers in user scope, and consider whether a broad server should be replaced by a narrower internal one exposing only the operations you actually use.",
k:"Every connected MCP server enlarges every agent's toolset. Scope servers per agent role; connections are not free."},

{i:210,d:2,t:"2.5",s:"DPT",diff:1,
q:"You need to find every file in the repository whose name ends in <code>.test.tsx</code>.",
ask:"Which built-in tool is correct?",
a:["<code>Glob</code>",
"<code>Grep</code>",
"<code>Read</code>",
"<code>Bash</code> with <code>find</code>"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. <code>Glob</code> finds files by name and extension pattern &mdash; exactly this job.",
"<code>Grep</code> searches file <em>contents</em>, not filenames.",
"<code>Read</code> opens a known file; it cannot discover files.",
"It would work, but it bypasses the purpose-built tool, is slower, and needlessly invokes shell capability."],
e:"Know the built-in tool boundaries cold; the exam tests them directly. <strong>Glob</strong>: find files by name or extension pattern. <strong>Grep</strong>: search within file contents for symbols, error messages, imports. <strong>Read</strong>: open a known file, ideally with an offset and limit. <strong>Write</strong>: create or fully replace a file. <strong>Edit</strong>: precise change via a unique text match. <strong>Bash</strong>: everything else &mdash; and it is the broadest, most dangerous grant, so reach for it last.",
k:"Glob = find files by name pattern. Grep = search contents. Read = open known file. Edit = unique-match change. Bash = last resort."},

{i:211,d:2,t:"2.5",s:"DPT",diff:1,
q:"You need to find every place in the codebase where the function <code>calculateTax</code> is called.",
ask:"Which built-in tool is correct?",
a:["<code>Grep</code>",
"<code>Glob</code>",
"<code>Read</code>",
"<code>Edit</code>"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. Searching file contents for a symbol is exactly what <code>Grep</code> does.",
"<code>Glob</code> matches filenames, and the function name is not in the filenames.",
"<code>Read</code> requires knowing which files to open, which is the question being asked.",
"<code>Edit</code> modifies files; it is not a search tool."],
e:"<code>Grep</code> is the content-search tool: function names, error messages, imports, configuration keys. It is also the correct <em>opening move</em> for codebase investigation &mdash; grep to locate candidates cheaply, then read only the specific files and ranges that matter. Remember the completeness caveat: a plain symbol search misses indirect calls through wrappers and re-exports, so trace those explicitly when the question is &ldquo;every caller&rdquo;.",
k:"Grep for contents, then Read the narrow set it identifies. Watch for indirect calls through wrappers when completeness matters."},

{i:212,d:2,t:"2.5",s:"DPT",diff:2,
q:"An <code>Edit</code> operation fails because the text you are trying to replace appears three times in the file.",
ask:"What is the correct fallback?",
a:["Read the file, then use Write to replace it with the corrected content &mdash; or expand the match string until it is unique",
"Run the Edit three times to change all occurrences",
"Delete the file and recreate it from memory",
"Use Bash with sed to force the replacement"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. The blueprint names Read + Write as the documented fallback when a unique match is not achievable.",
"The Edit already failed; repeating it fails identically, and blind repetition would change occurrences you did not intend.",
"Recreating from memory risks losing content that was never read.",
"Would work, but it reaches for the broadest tool to solve a problem the intended fallback handles safely."],
e:"<code>Edit</code> requires a unique text match, which is a safety property &mdash; it prevents an ambiguous change from silently hitting the wrong occurrence. The two correct responses are to expand the match string with surrounding context until it is unique, or to fall back to Read + Write for a full-file replacement. The blueprint names the second explicitly. Note that Write requires having read the file first, which is what makes the fallback safe.",
k:"Edit needs a unique match. Fallback: expand the match with context, or Read + Write the whole file. Not sed, not repetition."},

{i:213,d:2,t:"2.5",s:"DPT",diff:2,
q:"An agent is asked to understand how a request flows from an HTTP route to the database in an unfamiliar service.",
ask:"Which investigation strategy is most efficient?",
a:["Grep for the route definition, Read that handler, then follow each call it makes &mdash; building understanding incrementally",
"Read every file in the service directory in order",
"Glob for all files and read the ten largest",
"Ask the agent to describe the typical structure of such a service"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. Incremental investigation: cheap search to find the entry point, then targeted reads that follow the actual call chain.",
"Exhaustive reading burns context on files irrelevant to this particular flow.",
"File size is uncorrelated with relevance to a specific request path.",
"Answering from general patterns rather than from the code is exactly the ungrounded-answer failure."],
e:"The blueprint calls this incremental investigation: grep entry points, then read to trace flows. The pattern generalises &mdash; find the entry point cheaply, read it, identify its immediate dependencies, follow those, and stop when you can explain the flow. Each read is justified by something you found in the previous step, which keeps context spend proportional to the question rather than to the size of the repository.",
k:"Incremental investigation: grep the entry point &rarr; read it &rarr; follow its calls. Each read justified by the previous finding."},

{i:214,d:2,t:"2.5",s:"DPT",diff:3,
q:"You need to trace how a value flows from a public API function through a wrapper module to its eventual consumers.",
ask:"What must the investigation account for?",
a:["Indirect usage through re-exports and wrappers, since a direct symbol search will miss callers that go through the intermediary",
"That the wrapper module is probably dead code",
"That only the public API function matters, since wrappers are implementation detail",
"That Grep cannot search more than one directory at a time"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. Wrappers and re-exports break the direct symbol chain, so tracing must follow the intermediary's own exported names.",
"An unjustified assumption &mdash; wrappers exist precisely because something uses them.",
"The wrappers are how most callers reach the function, so ignoring them misses most of the usage.",
"Grep searches recursively across the tree."],
e:"The blueprint names tracing function usage through wrapper modules as an expected skill. The method: grep the original symbol to find direct callers <em>and</em> the wrapper that re-exports it, then grep the wrapper's exported name to find the indirect callers, repeating if there are several layers. Report coverage honestly &mdash; state which layers were traced &mdash; rather than presenting a partial list as exhaustive.",
k:"Trace through wrappers: grep the symbol, find re-exports, then grep the re-exported name. Repeat per layer and state your coverage."},

{i:215,d:2,t:"2.5",s:"DPT",diff:2,
q:"An agent needs to create a new file that does not yet exist.",
ask:"Which tool applies?",
a:["<code>Write</code>",
"<code>Edit</code>",
"<code>Read</code> then <code>Edit</code>",
"<code>Bash</code> with a heredoc"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. <code>Write</code> creates a new file or fully replaces an existing one.",
"<code>Edit</code> requires existing text to match against, and a new file has none.",
"There is nothing to read, since the file does not exist.",
"Would work, but it uses the broadest capability for something the purpose-built tool does directly and safely."],
e:"<code>Write</code> creates or fully replaces; <code>Edit</code> makes a precise change via a unique text match. The safety rule that follows: overwriting an existing file with <code>Write</code> should be preceded by a <code>Read</code>, so you are replacing content you have actually seen. Reaching for <code>Bash</code> when a purpose-built tool exists is a recurring wrong answer &mdash; it is broader capability than the task needs.",
k:"Write = create or full replace (Read first if it exists). Edit = precise unique-match change. Prefer purpose-built tools over Bash."},

{i:216,d:2,t:"2.1",s:"GEN",diff:2,
q:"Which of the following belongs in a tool description?",
ask:"Which element is most important to include?",
a:["The boundary against similar tools &mdash; what this tool is <em>not</em> for and which tool to use instead",
"The name of the team that maintains the tool",
"The tool's average response latency",
"The internal service the tool calls"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. Selection failures are usually near-misses between neighbouring tools, so the boundary does the most work.",
"Ownership is useful in your service catalogue, not in a description the model reads to choose.",
"Latency rarely drives correct selection and adds noise.",
"Implementation detail the model does not need and cannot act on."],
e:"A description exists to make a selection decision, so every element should serve that. The full checklist: what it does, when to choose it, input format with an example, what it returns, and the explicit boundary against the nearest alternatives. The boundary is the highest-value element in any system with more than a handful of tools, because that is where the errors concentrate.",
k:"Descriptions serve selection. The boundary against neighbours is the highest-value element; ownership and internals belong elsewhere."},

{i:217,d:2,t:"2.2",s:"GEN",diff:2,
q:"In MCP, which flag on a tool result indicates that the operation failed?",
ask:"Which is correct?",
a:["<code>isError</code>",
"<code>failed</code>",
"<code>status: \"error\"</code>",
"<code>success: false</code>"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. <code>isError</code> is the MCP flag marking a tool result as an error.",
"Not the MCP field name.",
"A plausible convention from other systems, but not the MCP flag.",
"Also plausible-looking, and also not the MCP flag."],
e:"A direct recall item. <code>isError</code> marks the result as a failure; the useful discipline is what you put alongside it. Structured metadata such as <code>errorCategory</code> (transient / validation / permission / business) and <code>isRetryable</code>, plus a specific human-readable message, is what lets the caller choose the right recovery. Note the related API-level field for the Messages API tool results is <code>is_error</code>.",
k:"MCP: <code>isError</code> flags a failed tool result. Pair it with <code>errorCategory</code>, <code>isRetryable</code> and a specific message."},

{i:218,d:2,t:"2.4",s:"GEN",diff:2,
q:"Which file defines MCP servers at project scope, shared with everyone who clones the repository?",
ask:"Which file is it?",
a:["<code>.mcp.json</code> in the project root",
"<code>~/.claude.json</code>",
"<code>.claude/settings.json</code>",
"<code>CLAUDE.md</code>"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. Project-scoped MCP server definitions live in <code>.mcp.json</code> and are version-controlled.",
"That is the user-scoped location, for personal and experimental servers.",
"Settings configure Claude Code behaviour and permissions, not MCP server definitions.",
"CLAUDE.md carries instructions and conventions, not configuration."],
e:"Keep the configuration files distinct, because the exam mixes them as distractors. <code>.mcp.json</code>: MCP server definitions at project scope, supporting <code>${ENV_VAR}</code> substitution for secrets. <code>~/.claude.json</code>: user-scoped MCP servers. <code>.claude/settings.json</code>: Claude Code settings and permissions. <code>CLAUDE.md</code>: project instructions and conventions. <code>.claude/rules/</code>: topic-focused, optionally path-scoped rule files.",
k:"<code>.mcp.json</code> = project MCP servers &middot; <code>~/.claude.json</code> = user MCP servers &middot; <code>settings.json</code> = behaviour and permissions &middot; CLAUDE.md = instructions."},

{i:219,d:2,t:"2.3",s:"GEN",diff:2,
q:"Which <code>tool_choice</code> value prevents the model from calling any tool on a given turn?",
ask:"Which is correct?",
a:["<code>{\"type\": \"none\"}</code>",
"<code>{\"type\": \"auto\"}</code>",
"<code>{\"type\": \"any\"}</code>",
"Omitting the <code>tools</code> parameter entirely"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. <code>none</code> forbids tool use for that request.",
"<code>auto</code> permits tool use; it merely does not require it.",
"<code>any</code> requires a tool call &mdash; the opposite.",
"It achieves the same practical effect, but it also removes the definitions from context, which matters if you intend to allow tools again on a later turn."],
e:"All four values are worth knowing precisely: <code>auto</code> (model decides), <code>any</code> (must call some tool), <code>{\"type\": \"tool\", \"name\": ...}</code> (must call that one), and <code>none</code> (no tools this turn). <code>none</code> is useful for a summarisation or explanation turn where you want prose and no side effects, while keeping the tool definitions in context for subsequent turns.",
k:"Four values: <code>auto</code>, <code>any</code>, forced <code>tool</code>, and <code>none</code>. Set per request; vary across the loop as the workflow requires."},

{i:220,d:2,t:"2.5",s:"GEN",diff:1,
q:"Which built-in tool makes a precise change to an existing file by matching unique text?",
ask:"Which is it?",
a:["<code>Edit</code>",
"<code>Write</code>",
"<code>Read</code>",
"<code>Glob</code>"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. <code>Edit</code> replaces a uniquely matching string, which keeps changes surgical.",
"<code>Write</code> creates or fully replaces a file rather than editing part of it.",
"<code>Read</code> only opens a file.",
"<code>Glob</code> finds files by name pattern."],
e:"<code>Edit</code>'s uniqueness requirement is a safety feature: an ambiguous match is rejected rather than silently applied to the wrong occurrence. When it fails, either expand the match string with surrounding context until it is unique, or fall back to Read + Write. Prefer <code>Edit</code> over <code>Write</code> for changes to existing files, since it touches only what you intend and produces a smaller, more reviewable diff.",
k:"Edit = surgical unique-match change (preferred for existing files). Write = create or full replace. Ambiguous match &rarr; expand context or Read + Write."}
);
